      en: "This division deepens practice that “dwells nowhere”: a bodhisattva should give without dwelling on any dharma — not on form, nor on sound, smell, taste, touch, or mental objects. If giving still clings to the six sense-objects — beauty or ugliness, praise or blame, pleasant or foul smell, rich or thin taste, comfort or pain, right or wrong concepts — merit stays finite; only “giving without dwelling on marks” yields merit beyond measure. The Buddha likens this to empty space in the ten directions, asking whether east, south, west, north, above, or below can be measured — Subhūti answers no each time — and says unattached giving’s merit is likewise immeasurable. The point is not how much is given or how prestigious the recipient, but whether the mind is clear and unclinging in the act: not seeking return, not seeking fame, not building a self-image as “the giver.” The closing line — abide just as taught — folds this unattached giving back into how the mind should abide: how to abide? Abide by not dwelling."
