      en: "Subhūti said to the Buddha: World-Honored One, will there be beings who, hearing these words and phrases, give rise to true faith? The Buddha told Subhūti: Do not speak this way. Five hundred years after the Tathāgata's passing, there will be those who keep the precepts and cultivate merit, who will give rise to faith in these words and take them as true. Know that such people have not planted good roots under just one Buddha, two Buddhas, three, four, or five Buddhas, but have already planted good roots under countless thousands of millions of Buddhas. When they hear these words and give rise to even a single thought of pure faith, Subhūti, the Tathāgata fully knows and fully sees that these beings gain such immeasurable merit. Why? These beings no longer hold notions of self, person, being, or life span; nor do they hold a notion of dharma, nor a notion of no-dharma. Why? If the minds of these beings grasp at any mark, they would be clinging to self, person, being, and life span. If they grasp at the mark of dharma, they would likewise cling to self, person, being, and life span. If they grasp at the mark of no-dharma, they would also cling to self, person, being, and life span. For this reason one should not grasp at dharmas, nor grasp at no-dharmas. This is why the Tathāgata always teaches: You monks, know that my teaching is like a raft — even the Dharma should be let go, how much more so what is not the Dharma."
